<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- No App Sandbox, deliberately.
	
	     This is the entitlement decision the whole product depends on, so it is
	     written down rather than left as an absence.
	
	     The sandbox redirects `~/Library` into a per-app container. `AutoStart`
	     writes a LaunchAgent to `~/Library/LaunchAgents`, so under the sandbox it
	     wrote to
	     `~/Library/Containers/<bundle>/Data/Library/LaunchAgents/` — a path
	     launchd never reads. The call succeeded, the log said "registered to
	     start at login", and nothing started at login. It had never worked.
	
	     The deeper reason is what this app is. It posts keyboard and mouse events
	     into other applications and captures the screen; driving other apps is
	     the category of thing the sandbox exists to prevent, and Apple does not
	     accept such apps on the Mac App Store, which is the only distribution
	     channel the sandbox is required for. Keeping it bought no security the
	     Accessibility and Screen Recording grants do not already gate, and cost a
	     feature outright.
	
	     What replaces it: Developer ID signing, the hardened runtime, and
	     notarisation — see `docs/PACKAGING.md`. The user still has to grant
	     Accessibility and Screen Recording by hand, and those grants are bound to
	     the code signature.
	
	     One migration consequence: application support moves out of the container
	     to `~/Library/Application Support/RemoteLink`, so an installation that
	     ran a sandboxed build has to pair its phones once more. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.network.client</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.network.server</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
